---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';
import { getPracticeURL, withPracticeURL } from '../lib/practice';
import checkBroken from '../samples/check-broken.txt?raw';
import checkFixed from '../samples/check-fixed.txt?raw';
import doctor from '../samples/doctor-scan.txt?raw';
import elementsButton from '../samples/sr-elements-button.txt?raw';
import expectFail from '../samples/sr-expect-fail.txt?raw';
import srStart from '../samples/sr-start.txt?raw';

const practiceURL = getPracticeURL(Astro);
const toc = [
   { id: 'install', label: '1. Install' },
   { id: 'doctor', label: '2. Check your machine' },
   { id: 'scan', label: '3. Scan a page' },
   { id: 'listen', label: '4. List what a screen reader announces' },
   { id: 'assert', label: '5. Test a dialog with a screen reader' },
   { id: 'stop', label: '6. Stop the session' },
   { id: 'fixed', label: '7. Compare the fixed page' },
   { id: 'next', label: 'Next steps' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Quickstart"
   summary="<p>Install a11ied, scan a page, and capture what a screen reader announces on it. This takes about five minutes. You need Node.js and Chrome, Edge, Brave, or Chromium.</p>"
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section>
      <h2 id="install">1. Install</h2>
      <pre><code>npm install --global a11ied</code></pre>
      <p>
         This installs one command under two names, <code>a1</code> and <code>a11ied</code>.
         These docs use <code>a1</code>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="doctor">2. Check your machine</h2>
      <pre><code>a1 doctor --task scan</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{doctor.trimEnd()}</code></pre>
      <p>
         A check mark (✓) at the start of the first line means you are ready. If the line
         starts with a cross (✗), run each command listed under
         <strong>Action items</strong>, then run the check again.
      </p>
      <p>
         This checks the two things the quickstart needs: a browser, and the simulated
         screen reader, <code>virtual-dom</code> in the list. VoiceOver and NVDA need more
         setup. You can do that later.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="scan">3. Scan a page</h2>
      <p>
         The docs serve a <a href="/demo/team">practice page</a>: a demo page with ten
         accessibility problems built in. Scan it.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 check {practiceURL}</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{withPracticeURL(checkBroken, practiceURL)}</code></pre>
      <p>
         The scan counts failing elements: ten of them, under three rules. So it found three
         of the page's ten problems. The other seven take more than a scan.
      </p>
      <p>Read the report in three parts.</p>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt>Problems</dt>
         <dd>What the scan found. Each entry gives the WCAG criterion that fails and its level (<code>[A]</code>, <code>[AA]</code>, or <code>[AAA]</code>), the rule, the failing elements, and a command that looks the rule up. A criterion with nothing under it, such as 1.4.6, fails by the rule listed just above it.</dd>
         <dt>Check by hand</dt>
         <dd>A checklist of the criteria on this page that need a manual test. A criterion can be in both lists: the scan found one failure, and the rest of the criterion still needs a person.</dd>
         <dt>The page</dt>
         <dd>What the page is made of. Two headings at levels 1 and 3 means a level was skipped.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         If the command prints an error and no report, see
         <a href="/guides/troubleshooting#load">the page did not load</a>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="listen">4. List what a screen reader announces</h2>
      <p>
         The scan says three buttons have no text. To find out what a screen reader
         announces for them, start a session.
      </p>
      <p class="note">
         Always pass <code>--sr virtual</code>, here and on the <code>a1 sr walk</code>
         command the report suggests. Without it, a Mac that is set up for VoiceOver starts
         VoiceOver, which takes control of the screen until you run <code>a1 sr stop</code>.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr start --sr virtual {practiceURL}</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{withPracticeURL(srStart, practiceURL)}</code></pre>
      <p>
         A session is a running screen reader. Every <code>a1 sr</code> command after this one
         controls it. Now list the buttons as the screen reader announces them.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr elements button</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{elementsButton.trimEnd()}</code></pre>
      <p>
         Buttons 5, 6, and 7 announce as "button" and nothing else. They remove a team member,
         and a screen reader user cannot tell. The fix is a name on each one, such as
         <code>aria-label="Remove Dana Whitfield"</code>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="assert">5. Test a dialog with a screen reader</h2>
      <p>
         The <strong>Invite member</strong> button opens a dialog. A screen reader should
         announce the dialog when it opens. Move to the button, mark the spot in the
         transcript, press the button, and check what was announced after the mark.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr goto --role button --name "Invite member"
a1 sr checkpoint opened
a1 sr activate
a1 sr expect dialog --since opened</code></pre>
      <p>
         The first three commands each print the element under the cursor, followed by three
         lines that end in <code>unsupported</code> or <code>unavailable</code>. Those lines
         are normal for the simulated screen reader. The last command prints this:
      </p>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{expectFail.trimEnd()}</code></pre>
      <p>
         The dialog opened on screen and the screen reader said nothing. The cursor stayed on
         the button, so a screen reader user does not know a dialog exists. A static scan cannot
         report this.
      </p>
      <p>
         <code>sr expect</code> exits with code 4 when the announcement is missing, so the same
         four lines work as a check in a script or in CI.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="stop">6. Stop the session</h2>
      <pre><code>a1 sr stop</code></pre>
      <p>A session that you forget to stop ends after 30 idle minutes.</p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="fixed">7. Compare the fixed page</h2>
      <p>
         The same page with all ten problems repaired is at <code>{practiceURL}fixed/</code>.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 check {practiceURL}fixed/</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{withPracticeURL(checkFixed, practiceURL)}</code></pre>
      <p>
         Nothing failed. The checklist of criteria to test by hand is longer here, because
         the fixed page adds a live region for its confirmation message and more criteria
         now apply. After a clean scan, that checklist is what to test next.
      </p>
      <p>Run steps 4 and 5 against the fixed page and <code>sr expect</code> passes.</p>
      <p>
         You have now scanned a page, heard a defect the way a screen reader user does, and
         written a check that fails when a dialog does not announce itself.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="next">Next steps</h2>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt>Your own page</dt>
         <dd>Run <code>a1 check</code> with the address of a page you are building, such as <code>http://localhost:3000/</code>.</dd>
         <dt><a href="/guides/demo">Practice on a broken page</a></dt>
         <dd>Find the other problems on this demo page, one command at a time.</dd>
         <dt><a href="/guides/screen-reader">Test with a screen reader</a></dt>
         <dd>Move, read, and check announcements with VoiceOver, NVDA, or the simulated screen reader.</dd>
         <dt><a href="/guides/vitest">Test with Vitest</a></dt>
         <dd>Turn step 5 into a test that runs with the rest of your suite.</dd>
         <dt><a href="/guides/agents">Use with an AI agent</a></dt>
         <dd>Give a coding agent these commands as tools.</dd>
      </dl>
   </section>
</DocShell>
